<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Audumbara</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 100 CE (approx.) · capital Pathankot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A people of the Punjab hills whose coins carry some of the earliest images of a Hindu temple.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Audumbaras are mentioned by the grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> and later texts as a people of the north-west. Coins show that by the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE they formed a state in the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathankot">Pathankot</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known beyond their coinage. Their coins, with legends in Brahmi and Kharoshthi, name rulers such as Dharaghosha, Shivadasa and Rudradasa, and some show a pillared shrine beside a trident, one of the earliest depictions of a temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The emphasis on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the trident points to a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> cult, and the name comes from the udumbara fig tree.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Audumbaras appear to have lost their independence to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> in the 1st century, after which their coinage ends.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 200 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.72%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pathankot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dharaghosha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Audumbaras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=audumbara" aria-label="Audumbara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6625 1004 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Audumbara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6945 1260L6757 1168"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6945 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6945 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pathankot. <a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=audumbara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

